Riders in History and          NannyMUD Times                         Page 9
Modern Times

  
  Since ancient times humanity has fought wars with horses.
  The Roman Equites, the Ordo Equester, ranking below the
  patrician class, but still aristocratic to the Hippeis,
  the Greek equivalent of the second highest Athenian social class.
  Equus meaning horse, Hippo the same in Greek.
  The riders, knights, cavaliers were historically of those
  noble classes who could afford horses.
  
  The Swedish word Riddare simply means rider,
  the Cavalier transformed into the modern South American
  Caballero, the cowboys of current times.
  
  Modern fiction:
  I have played many games with knights
  though usually they do not have any horses, and if they do
  they do not have any mounted combat.
  
  This can seem a bit odd to the historians among us
  as a knight, a riddare, a cavalier is a warrior mounted on a horse
  in warfare and in peace.
  In Monty Python and the Holy Grail they could not afford to have horses
  so they came up with the age old tradition of banging coconuts together
  getting many jokes out of this even though as all of us
  who have ridden horses know that coconuts doesn't sound anything
  like coconuts banged together.
  
  Modern game heroes such as Link of the Zelda series with his trusty
  steed Epona is more similar to the Western and Japanese ideal
  of a noble, mounted and brave hero, fighting on horseback for freedom,
  justice and against the forces of evil with sword, shield and bow.
  
  This is the essence of what a knight should be, pure, brave, honourable,
  fighting for what is right and to protect the innocent and spread hope
  in the darkness.
  Maybe what matters is not what the knight rides, but what is in his
  mind, heart and soul, the ideals and virtues he practice.
  
  For honour and glory.
  Hisashi.
